  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Home Server",
    "overview": "The control panel should feel like one more server in the owner's Discord sidebar. It's the place their bot lives: dark, calm and immediately familiar to anyone who uses Discord every day. The sidebar reads as a channel list, the bottom-left panel reads as Discord's user panel showing the bot's presence, and settings sit on flat cards in Discord's charcoal greys. A self-hoster opening it after hours in the tray should know where everything is before they read a word.\n\nThe language is Discord's, tightened slightly into a tool. Discord's settings screens are spacious, but this app holds sliders, tables, logs and model forms, so spacing stays compact (a 4px base, 16px card padding, 10px between items) and hierarchy comes from weight and colour rather than size. Depth comes from small steps in lightness within one cool grey hue, never from outlines or shadows. Blurple is the only accent, and the presence colours (green, amber, red, grey) mean bot state and nothing else.\n\nThe look this system replaced is the one thing it rejects outright: cards, tables and lists boxed in solid `#35373c` borders. No emoji appear in the interface. Tone comes from colour, and every glyph is monochrome.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Discord-native dark UI: a channel-list sidebar, a user-panel status block, flat cards.",
      "One accent (Blurple), used only for primary actions, selection, focus and toggles.",
      "Depth comes from lightness steps within one hue. No borders on cards and no shadows.",
      "Presence colours describe bot state and are never decorative.",
      "Compact, tool-like density with weight-led hierarchy.",
      "No emoji. Monochrome glyphs and small SVG icons only."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Accent Rule",
        "body": "Blurple is the only accent colour. If something needs to stand out and it isn't a primary action, selection, focus or a toggle, use weight or `text-strong`, not colour.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Presence Means State Rule",
        "body": "Green, amber, red and grey on a status element always mean the bot's connection state. Never use them for decoration, categories or emphasis.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Weight Before Size Rule",
        "body": "Build hierarchy with weight and colour first and size second. The full range runs from 11px to 16px. A new screen shouldn't need anything bigger.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Sink, Don't Outline Rule",
        "body": "To separate an element, step its surface up or down one level. Never draw a border around a card, table or list. That was the old look.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Hairline Only Between Regions Rule",
        "body": "1px `hairline` dividers belong between the sidebar, header and status bar, and nowhere inside the content area.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** take every colour from `TOKENS` in `gui/theme.py`. Add a token there before you use a new value.",
      "**Do** separate surfaces by stepping lightness (`base` → `card` → `inset` → `well`), following the Sink, Don't Outline Rule.",
      "**Do** give every button a disabled state, and enable bot-dependent actions only while `BotState` is ONLINE. Disable them on CONNECTING, STOPPING, DISCONNECTED and ERROR as appropriate.",
